{"name":"Radiation Carrot","hex":"#ffa343","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"radiation","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The shooting forth of anything from a point or surface, like diverging rays of light.","Ellipsis of electromagnetic radiation."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/radiation"},{"term":"carrot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vegetable with a nutritious, juicy, sweet root that is often orange in colour, Daucus carota, family Apiaceae, especially the subspecies sativus.","A shade of orange similar to the flesh of most carrots (also called carrot orange)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To treat (an animal pelt) with a solution of mercuric nitrate as part of felt manufacture."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carrot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radiation","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, radiation is the emission or transmission of energy in the form of waves or particles through space or a material medium. This includes:"},{"items":["electromagnetic radiation consisting of photons, such as radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, x-rays, and gamma radiation (γ)","particle radiation consisting of particles of non-zero rest energy, such as alpha radiation (α), beta radiation (β), proton radiation and neutron radiation","acoustic radiation, such as ultrasound, sound, and seismic waves, all dependent on a physical transmission medium","gravitational radiation, in the form of gravitational waves, ripples in spacetime"]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Alfa_beta_gamma_radiation_penetration.svg/330px-Alfa_beta_gamma_radiation_penetration.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radiation"},{"title":"Carrot","blocks":[{"text":"The carrot is a root vegetable in the umbellifer family Apiaceae. Carrots are typically orange in colour due to their beta-carotene content, which makes them a rich source of vitamin A. Heirloom variants may be white, yellow, red, purple, or black. They are biennial plants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Vegetable-Carrot-Bundle-wStalks.jpg/330px-Vegetable-Carrot-Bundle-wStalks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrot"},{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png/500px-Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png/1280px-Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png/500px-Details_of_types_of_Radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADetails_of_types_of_Radiation.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg/500px-Radon_decay_in_a_cloud_chamber.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut